; STORE-SHELL §5, the ORDER of checks 2 and 3 (ruling W3-13) — R-C §3.6's
; `19-canon-diagnosis`, the corrected form of `r2-14-canon-involution-self-reject`.
;
; F-41, the other direction of F-40's root. `schema-put <carrier>` assembles bytes with
; the core's OWN canonicalizing `preimageS`. On a NON-palindromic duplicate-key run
; `canonS` is an involution, so the boundary's re-canonicalization disagreed with the
; assembly and the shell rejected bytes it had itself produced from a carrier literal —
; with the verdict `non-canonical`, which was not a true statement about the carrier.
;
; W3-13's argument, in one line: `ObligationCanonIdempotent` is CONDITIONAL on `dupFreeS`,
; so outside that hypothesis the byte-compare's verdict is not a statement about
; canonicity at all — it is the involution leaking. Well-formedness first restores the
; hypothesis, and `non-canonical` goes back to meaning what §5 says it means.
;
; THE ASSERTION THAT MATTERS IS THE REJECTION STRING, not the exit code: both orders
; reject these two shapes with code 1. What flipped is the diagnosis, from
; `non-canonical` to `not-well-formed dup-key`, and a reader can now tell an F-40 fix
; from a canonicity regression.

; the non-palindromic pair: int then str at one key
(schema-put (object (f "a" req (prim int)) (f "a" req (prim str))))
(assert-code prev 1)

; a non-palindromic run carried beside a distinct key
(schema-put (object (f "b" req (prim int)) (f "a" req (prim int)) (f "a" req (prim str))))
(assert-code prev 1)

; the ordering change must be INVISIBLE to every dup-free input, and this is the leg that
; says so from the other side: genuine non-canonicity still reports as non-canonicity.
; `schema-put-raw` assembles a pre-image with canonicalization deliberately skipped, so
; the fields reach the boundary out of order with no duplicate key anywhere. Check 2
; passes, check 3 rejects. (Script 08 is the standing regression test for this property
; and must stay green UNCHANGED — if it ever changes, the ordering wiring over-reached.)
(schema-put-raw (object (f "beta" req (prim str)) (f "alpha" req (prim int))))
(assert-code prev 1)

; nothing landed.
(check)
